    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI extended the business-day rule for the registration date to also skip public holidays, in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (the single place this logic lives):\n\n1. **Added the fixed public-holiday list** as a constant:\n```java\nprivate static final Set<LocalDate> PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS = Set.of(\n    LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 1),\n    LocalDate.of(2026, 1, 26),\n    LocalDate.of(2026, 4, 25),\n    LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 25),\n    LocalDate.of(2026, 12, 28));\n```\n\n2. **Updated `toBusinessDay`** so the roll-forward loop treats a listed holiday like a weekend, advancing one day at a time until it lands on a non-weekend, non-holiday day. This correctly handles chained cases too (e.g. 2026-12-25 Fri \u2192 skips Sat/Sun and the 12-28 Mon holiday \u2192 2026-12-29):\n```java\nprivate LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate date) {\n    while (isWeekend(date) || PUBLIC_HOLIDAYS.contains(date)) {\n        date = date.plusDays(1);\n    }\n    return date;\n}\n```\n\nThe adjusted date continues to flow through everything derived from it (membership number year segment, daily create-limit, etc.), since the adjustment happens at the single point where the registration date is set. My holiday set matches the test-side reference (`AcceptanceBase.HOLIDAYS`) exactly.\n\n`./mvnw -o test` passes: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**",
